       wwaaddddcchh puts the character _c_h at the cursor position of window _w_i_n, then
       advances  the  cursor position, analogously to the standard C library's
       _p_u_t_c_h_a_r(3).  nnccuurrsseess(3X) describes the variants of this function.

       If advancement occurs at the right margin,

       •   the cursor automatically wraps to the beginning of the  next  line;
           and

       •   at  the bottom of the current scrolling region, and if ssccrroollllookk(3X)
           is enabled for _w_i_n, the scrolling region scrolls up one line.

       If _c_h is a backspace, carriage return, line feed, or  tab,  the  cursor
       moves appropriately within the window.

       •   Backspace  moves  the cursor one character left; at the left margin
           of a window, it does nothing.

       •   Carriage return moves the cursor to the left margin on the  current
           line of the window.

       •   Line  feed  does  a ccllrrttooeeooll(3X), then moves the cursor to the left
           margin on the next line of the window, and if ssccrroollllookk(3X)  is  en‐
           abled  for _w_i_n, scrolls the window if the cursor was already on the
           last line.

       •   Tab advances the cursor to the next tab stop (possibly on the  next
           line);  these  are placed at every eighth column by default.  Alter
           the   tab   interval    with    the    TTAABBSSIIZZEE    extension;    see
           ccuurrss__vvaarriiaabblleess(3X).

       If  _c_h  is  any  other nonprintable character, it is drawn in printable
       form, using the same convention as uunnccttrrll(3X).

       Calling wwiinncchh(3X) on the location of a nonprintable character does  not
       return the character itself, but its uunnccttrrll(3X) representation.

       _c_h  may  contain  rendering  and/or color attributes, and others can be
       combined with the parameter by logically “or”ing with it.  (A character
       with  its  attributes can be copied from place to place using wwiinncchh(3X)
       and wwaaddddcchh.)  See ccuurrss__aattttrr(3X) for values of predefined  video  attri‐
